#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172085 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171891 :question/id #uuid "bccee2b9-f937-5df5-b3cf-4e45096c9ac3" :question/math-academy-id "e-9465" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^2?$\n\n- $\\mathbf A(x,y) = \\sqrt{x}\\,\\mathbf i + y^3\\,\\mathbf j$\n\n- $\\mathbf B(x,y,z) = \\ln y\\,\\mathbf i - (x+y)\\,\\mathbf j  + z\\,\\mathbf k$\n\n- $\\mathbf C(t) = \\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf i + (2-t^3)\\,\\mathbf j$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^2.$ A vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ is a function $\\mathbf F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $\\mathbf F(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $\\mathbf A(x,y)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\:|\\: x \\geq 0\\big\\}\n$$\n\nto a vector in $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\n- The function $\\mathbf B(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2.$ It is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$\n\n- The function $\\mathbf C(t)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2.$  It assigns points in $\\mathbb{R}$ (not $\\mathbb{R^2}$) to vectors in $\\mathbb{R^2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "019e5a17-1aab-565b-8881-447c86a9a7ec" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049814 :topic/math-academy-id 1849 :topic/title "Vectors in N-Dimensional Euclidean Space"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050992 :topic/math-academy-id 1899 :topic/title "The Domain of a Multivariable Function"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172278 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15deb0f8-c55d-57a2-81f6-2142722bdf77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253860} {:answer/id #uuid "178907e3-8b45-599f-b8d0-3349646c4f94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253873} {:answer/id #uuid "38438f74-0aa6-57ff-b661-78f5f62ca8ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254157} {:answer/id #uuid "4f2f9fa9-ddf1-5a6b-92cb-311164a1df91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254357} {:answer/id #uuid "f4597c3d-7459-57b3-a142-69729b5c48f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255809}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38438f74-0aa6-57ff-b661-78f5f62ca8ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254157} :answer-field/id #uuid "4824be83-0ec4-51e9-874c-98cf1bb977d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256060}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2d1a20d-340e-5980-ad18-92b97e0270ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-122465" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $G(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j$\n- $H(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,i-{e}^{xy}\\,j$\n- $K(x,y,z)=x\\,i-y\\,j+z\\,k$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $G(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ to a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $H(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y>0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $K(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172283 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5580f132-72e3-58fb-813f-49af468ded0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254416} {:answer/id #uuid "5db5413c-3f34-5816-961d-b448ce4b9db0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254501} {:answer/id #uuid "b87b61e9-267d-5c02-8af5-6433b7ac5186" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255308} {:answer/id #uuid "deaa0d07-6189-57b9-b848-94156c8b1a2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255646} {:answer/id #uuid "f992c0e9-523e-5294-8097-50964bbbe2db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "deaa0d07-6189-57b9-b848-94156c8b1a2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255646} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9b70b21-828a-5bbd-8dd0-9c7ef4077bf2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256278}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7b23ed53-bb63-5272-af53-a6429d08e4c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-122643" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $P(x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,i+(x+y)^{2}\\,j$\n- $Q(x,y,z)=y\\,i-xy\\,j+(z+1)\\,k$\n- $R(t)=(t+1)\\,i-(3t-1)\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ne 0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $Q(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $R(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It assigns points in  $R$ (not ${R}^{2}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256685 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7dcdb238-84f5-50d1-81fb-d46208d9425f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254783} {:answer/id #uuid "94b9519b-6e8f-5cb8-8040-62148a141e85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254977} {:answer/id #uuid "a70688af-7165-571e-8ce0-430e79655143" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255143} {:answer/id #uuid "b62432e8-8719-5cba-8d52-19c52dce26b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255281} {:answer/id #uuid "f2f16d7b-50db-57c4-829a-2c1c1353919f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255794}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a70688af-7165-571e-8ce0-430e79655143" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255143} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c523d76-2d1e-5e8c-9b53-1b2e99146620" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255934}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "82c19ae8-c5db-5680-ac18-77dd108b7959" :question/math-academy-id "q-122650" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $U(x,y)=⟨\\frac{1}{x+2},\\sqrt{x-y}⟩$\n- $V(x,y)=⟨x{e}^{y},1-y⟩$\n- $W(x,y,z)=⟨x,-y,-z⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $U(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ne -2,\\,x-y\\ge 0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $V(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ to a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $W(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256687 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c408810-efc6-5638-a618-d28745a9b48a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253767} {:answer/id #uuid "4849dc08-25ab-5a55-b530-c3ded2927c4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254287} {:answer/id #uuid "a6b8d887-618e-5d51-b874-9c8b99a43860" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255139} {:answer/id #uuid "a90bb982-3a8e-52d4-919e-59317069be3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255159} {:answer/id #uuid "b7e3eaf3-da05-5aa5-b7ef-f5fd16504087" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4849dc08-25ab-5a55-b530-c3ded2927c4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254287} :answer-field/id #uuid "321403c5-6d4b-5e0e-88e6-f677e7259264" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256009}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "212db81f-3e39-5c00-88aa-953a8a56ffb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-122653" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $A(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y),\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)⟩$\n- $B(x,y,z)=⟨-2x,3{y}^{3},{z}^{5}⟩$\n- $C(t)=⟨2-{t}^{2},{e}^{t-1}⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $A(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x+y>0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $B(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $C(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$  It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{2}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256693 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49a2f5e2-8b9e-5771-8e4c-130ad318d884" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254303} {:answer/id #uuid "740aab86-ef29-5671-b4b8-e9ccd3a8d22e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254696} {:answer/id #uuid "832c1dab-f56a-57a4-bb5b-347d7aa89125" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254836} {:answer/id #uuid "d057ce44-c181-5fc1-a138-ce9005660990" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255525} {:answer/id #uuid "fd43e487-e4ea-56ad-b5c6-843be58a5b74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255896}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d057ce44-c181-5fc1-a138-ce9005660990" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255525} :answer-field/id #uuid "b10ade94-378e-5853-8e3a-658322f792bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256242}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f63ae22-12f3-552d-bede-acf1007c6564" :question/math-academy-id "q-122678" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $U(x,y)=⟨(x+2){e}^{y},\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x-y}⟩$\n- $V(x,y,z)=⟨5,{x}^{3}y,1-{y}^{3}⟩$\n- $W(x,y)=⟨x-3{y}^{2},2-y⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $U(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ne y}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $K(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $V(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ to a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380657 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34c119eb-a2e9-5e7b-aece-194690e3575f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380659} {:answer/id #uuid "f11bcfb0-554a-553b-a2cd-9d076207bb59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380660} {:answer/id #uuid "7d17c850-0945-5f89-a53a-ffaee5e1a53e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380661} {:answer/id #uuid "04ec628e-d678-57c6-afb0-df8af8ce90b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380662} {:answer/id #uuid "649bb3e8-6868-569d-a178-c5ab91222bdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f11bcfb0-554a-553b-a2cd-9d076207bb59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380660} :answer-field/id #uuid "caef7be4-fa1f-5ecd-8432-0db8e2abf1fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380658}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1de1af2b-64e8-50cf-80c9-f414743fe7ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-122641" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $G(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j$\n- $H(t)=\\sqrt{t}\\,i+t{e}^{t}\\,j$\n- $K(x,y,z)=2x\\,i+y\\,j-3z\\,k$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $G(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ to a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $H(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$  It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{2}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $K(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380664 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16526cbf-eed9-5dc8-b49d-07c80cc53aee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380666} {:answer/id #uuid "76cc8d11-9c5e-5b66-857e-79223281043f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380667} {:answer/id #uuid "1bfe71e4-e268-5307-ad87-4f7d22a6a41f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380668} {:answer/id #uuid "85e5e6ca-2257-5462-aa74-7664e109c0ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380669} {:answer/id #uuid "d3ffeac8-d5ff-53ee-ad33-6d914cd947c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3ffeac8-d5ff-53ee-ad33-6d914cd947c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380670} :answer-field/id #uuid "21763b78-6aa3-55ed-be0a-c14268a02c40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380665}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "502cb71c-02d6-5e14-a31b-2cee28a0a5ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-122656" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $M(x,y)=⟨y-{x}^{2},3x+{y}^{2},\\frac{1}{x+y}⟩$\n- $N(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+1,1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x⟩$\n- $P(t)=⟨{t}^{3},\\sqrt{1-t}\\,⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $M(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It assigns points in  ${R}^{2}$ to vectors in ${R}^{3}$ (not in ${R}^{2}$).\n\n- The function $N(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ to a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $P(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It assigns points in  $R$ \n(not ${R}^{2}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380678 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d818659d-904f-56ab-a894-ba5c08da4a69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380680} {:answer/id #uuid "073a4146-a75e-5400-a120-20b8b4dcbe03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380681} {:answer/id #uuid "25a00a6a-d7e9-5e03-949c-8562fa76683a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380682} {:answer/id #uuid "60870366-832c-58f3-ab71-17ad52ae15cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380683} {:answer/id #uuid "4a72a1c4-87b7-54cc-8c12-34d1ba68b017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60870366-832c-58f3-ab71-17ad52ae15cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380683} :answer-field/id #uuid "cee31fa4-3f78-5e6b-b4f0-0888305b3c27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380679}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b352a16-fe6f-5946-bac9-7b334007947a" :question/math-academy-id "q-122675" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $C(x,y)=⟨\\frac{1}{x+y},{x}^{2}{e}^{y}⟩$\n- $D(x,y)=⟨{y}^{3}-3,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y)⟩$\n- $E(x,y)=⟨x+1,y+2,x-y⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $C(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x+y\\ne 0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $D(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ to a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $E(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ to vectors in ${R}^{3}$ (not ${R}^{2}$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields on Two-Dimensions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172086 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171892 :question/id #uuid "193765c8-ea2c-5d90-b902-09d6d44a3a67" :question/math-academy-id "e-9466" :question/problem "Find the domain of the vector field $\\mathbf F$ on $\\mathbb R^2,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) =\\dfrac{1}{x^2+y^2}\\, \\mathbf{i} + \\sqrt{1+x-y} \\, \\mathbf{j}\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $\\mathbf F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y) =\\dfrac{1}{x^2+y^2}, \\qquad Q(x,y) = \\sqrt{1+x-y} .\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $\\mathbf F\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx^2 + y^2 = 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow \\quad x=y = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nD_1 = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\: | \\: (x,y)\\neq (0,0)\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe region $D_1$ is the entire plane $\\mathbb R^2$ with the origin removed, as shown below.\n\n![](images/d4/d4a418bc0dbfc61c0e55c494db51ee0fa8125b6b7056ccbe4e32ddca82574dc2.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive or zero. To find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n1+x - y \\geq 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad y\\leq 1 + x\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $Q$ is given by\n\n$$\nD_2 = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\: | \\: y \\leq 1 + x\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe region $D_2,$ shown below:\n\n![](images/24/242903cc94103f8bb9cdc190a229fe7e86395f44600e8318d728fea3bc339385.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $\\mathbf F$ is given by \n\n$$\nD =  D_1\\cap D_2 = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\: | \\: (x,y)\\neq 0,\\, y \\leq 1 + x\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/f0/f02ca21443a81cdb039a3d97bab0b0f1036219bb89ecbdaa32386cbc4e5da6e3.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67b7ea90-38e0-54b5-b76e-816beb0d48f9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048942 :topic/math-academy-id 1930 :topic/title "Interior and Boundary Points"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172280 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "423fabcb-e2a7-5a29-9594-edebd6450c5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254235} {:answer/id #uuid "5f73033d-d5ff-5da8-8c1c-28671e834b1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254522} {:answer/id #uuid "74831d73-da33-5161-a77e-fdc419d47566" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y\\ne \\pm x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254699} {:answer/id #uuid "8bd3c407-37a0-54c9-8259-7c347f371005" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,(x,y)\\ne 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254904} {:answer/id #uuid "8dc43b23-9268-520e-aab5-397b548f35f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y\\ne x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254920}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74831d73-da33-5161-a77e-fdc419d47566" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y\\ne \\pm x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254699} :answer-field/id #uuid "7898097d-ceac-5c85-876e-b05ab6520f8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256142}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e58a710f-da20-5986-98eb-b29ea7b4485f" :question/math-academy-id "q-122474" :question/problem "Determine the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,j\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=y=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,(x,y)\\ne (0,0)}.\n$$\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\pm x\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $Q$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y\\ne \\pm x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y\\ne \\pm x}\\,.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172281 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "129904d8-64df-5ea9-8154-f524933d7832" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e1/e1d9695fa4da169a7ec9c1f3ff20db320212bce34047fc13bb27028aadc95d79.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253832} {:answer/id #uuid "6f7205d3-5a29-5421-97fb-b1fb12155b6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/30/30462ed1f25749ac4ef05cf9ca9573508ae28a0799d7986dd005f71fdac15679.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254657} {:answer/id #uuid "a17c075f-c15e-5440-a087-1a116b3c38b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c6/c61552b7b9d8c24e438a8fa2d0a9248a3938c8e3512ca4a012d8b3342dd47a0f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255093} {:answer/id #uuid "a546db51-b877-5a6b-b55a-3fb457be5c19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/23/237dbec35d2ea6556014373efccbf1d34c31404fde3a015feb3c433056c63a3a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255128} {:answer/id #uuid "ff640f01-fdc3-54ce-a2f5-a80d67136507" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/60/606f516cb05ba0922cc63cd672a0df864bea121d9c50982a50d3c6f61465f3bf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255917}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f7205d3-5a29-5421-97fb-b1fb12155b6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/30/30462ed1f25749ac4ef05cf9ca9573508ae28a0799d7986dd005f71fdac15679.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254657} :answer-field/id #uuid "c53c4cc1-6e27-599f-b5f7-0fbae5ebf382" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256268}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "46b9e86b-e188-5e02-ada4-a07528592166" :question/math-academy-id "q-122518" :question/problem "Determine which of the following shaded regions depicts the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<9.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{1},$ which is the interior of a circle of radius $3,$ as shown below:\n\n![](images/e5/e54618f7e72d70f299686ca2846a1d6fa0caccb8daecb090b3a653271c88fcd3.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=y=0.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{2},$ which is the entire plane ${R}^{2}$ with the origin removed:\n\n![](images/5b/5b77ad4e9574e94e6a05eee7680f4b57d0190d40583c37c2097acef958cab7e2.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by ${D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2},$ shown below.\n\n![](images/30/30462ed1f25749ac4ef05cf9ca9573508ae28a0799d7986dd005f71fdac15679.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256688 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56102ac4-0111-50bf-baa1-120344804ec0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x<y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254425} {:answer/id #uuid "76a03f33-46e2-5d1c-8929-cfef138e411e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ne y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254717} {:answer/id #uuid "c6fa9022-755b-5cfe-8215-5e56801bef47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,(x,y)\\ne (0,0),\\,x\\ge y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255447} {:answer/id #uuid "de3053b1-0e26-5159-83fc-ae08b0b64b4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,(x,y)\\ne (0,0)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255641} {:answer/id #uuid "eff20b85-4c4c-5b34-8add-3733f5aa6cba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255767}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eff20b85-4c4c-5b34-8add-3733f5aa6cba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255767} :answer-field/id #uuid "32eb6772-d2dc-5309-9bae-71a60e83b4a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256010}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9d82b252-32f5-5b9f-80ce-d3ae2209d9d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-122657" :question/problem "Determine the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)\\,i+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y),\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the logarithm is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\nx-y>0\\,⟹\\,x>y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>y}.\n$$\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=y=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $Q$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,(x,y)\\ne (0,0)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>y}\\,.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256691 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d0def04-0c36-58a0-a01f-2ed0f1bb2044" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4a/4a15607520f8ef05913aab5d1724c8b3158457e56d455b6eb756707263494308.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254070} {:answer/id #uuid "2d8a7024-c0df-5add-9db4-ef0e6d955c88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/26/26709ab7fa3cf1962ff9dca9be510cf5c24f3568f82347a8349e77dc5095fb4c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254077} {:answer/id #uuid "3285decd-3c25-54c5-a110-1f6bbe59e735" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a3/a3884061e6c5f8b5310a8b0bcf06ac844b12b418497d9f6e5b3beced6147d964.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254108} {:answer/id #uuid "5cacab87-de13-5e9a-892c-1c5b560e306c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0e/0e6843335d08bc42a95a5801aa683aeb2a043df0cd7edc03ffbc36a9385fee2a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254490} {:answer/id #uuid "f37057c0-c75a-55e4-bbb0-a9e433ba13c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/24/2469abf572b9b6fd9da28ae81951b99acdca9b97275af2bc0a96d3411d7fbea1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f37057c0-c75a-55e4-bbb0-a9e433ba13c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/24/2469abf572b9b6fd9da28ae81951b99acdca9b97275af2bc0a96d3411d7fbea1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255803} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc8fcbe1-118d-5975-9f5d-fda6bda82689" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256289}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "81ef4329-ef48-5a14-8457-2a79f1d3df80" :question/math-academy-id "q-122671" :question/problem "Determine which of the following shaded regions depicts the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive or equal to zero. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{1},$ which is the interior of a circle of radius $1,$ as shown below:\n\n![](images/9f/9f5f11a4ca31cb796f5a95a1541aa946e24fd1b7079ab8069e5743d41d92d764.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=y=0.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{2},$ which is the entire plane ${R}^{2}$ with the origin removed:\n\n![](images/5b/5b77ad4e9574e94e6a05eee7680f4b57d0190d40583c37c2097acef958cab7e2.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by ${D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2},$ shown below.\n\n![](images/95/95e8d137f90135de1727451fbc96ddfcc325e9e4461054615c91743a4a9ea54a.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256704 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23be673f-249f-54d5-8bba-1196cc91218d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/11/11cfd599bbfe85c9f3c7a23d5b95c6bd2b3ff199c6c0a4777429123afc74cb8c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253992} {:answer/id #uuid "2d5e44b9-db64-5b67-b712-489ffc553051" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ff/ff0774689ae7215b624ea7eb3bed8f68a673fba4bcc4ceeb73f82b9081c32d4f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254074} {:answer/id #uuid "8cf757cd-3613-5b80-9f15-fd5bdceadfc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4a/4aeebc183f9a6676f7487fce53ec46ad0f2cc91a87847d8a230471dc638ea448.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254915} {:answer/id #uuid "da8daeb9-2bcb-50c1-b44c-600775cb8bb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/de/de222a9251e08861dd06fdee593cc10557f0be1d2c7d891395512d699b35aa63.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255609} {:answer/id #uuid "e605afc3-fcef-5e1e-a9be-583c83c6dfe1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/49/49000dd6905017bc64c9a67b6d8a2ea475a684477ac6e02d6ed15c4c7dbbb3aa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e605afc3-fcef-5e1e-a9be-583c83c6dfe1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/49/49000dd6905017bc64c9a67b6d8a2ea475a684477ac6e02d6ed15c4c7dbbb3aa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255698} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b83f961-43ac-5ecd-a230-9fdb901ae8cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255998}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "977dd2df-2a75-5c51-b25e-8edbacd60b15" :question/math-academy-id "q-122710" :question/problem "Determine which of the following shaded regions depicts the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-{x}^{2})\\,i+x\\sqrt{1-|y|}\\,j\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-{x}^{2}),\\,Q(x,y)=x\\sqrt{1-|y|}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the logarithm is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n1-{x}^{2}>0\\,⟹\\,-1<x<1.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{1},$ shown below:\n\n![](images/89/89c78ec587826c15c90ef4ffdfc0fd9f8af3d7b4d3d1cc099ce24b883b9842c2.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined the expression under the radical is positive or equal to zero. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n1-|y|\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,-1\\le y\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{2},$ shown below:\n\n![](images/ad/addd649aa739711fd34ebd3e82579c8c0100a2b9a109d5cafabde750bc94eb9a.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by ${D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2},$ shown below.\n\n![](images/49/49000dd6905017bc64c9a67b6d8a2ea475a684477ac6e02d6ed15c4c7dbbb3aa.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380671 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8cc3763-c596-539c-84ff-fc43a34ca838" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<4,\\,x\\ne 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380673} {:answer/id #uuid "e393beba-f44d-54af-a58f-f17495b74529" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380674} {:answer/id #uuid "d5a628af-d843-5691-850a-a01497b612f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 4,\\,x\\ne 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380675} {:answer/id #uuid "5ea6c947-7540-5f8e-9661-c097d1519263" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380676} {:answer/id #uuid "6105c7e2-8e73-526d-b477-10f7ed9a3aac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5a628af-d843-5691-850a-a01497b612f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 4,\\,x\\ne 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380675} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ced28f1-8592-507f-b2f3-ca4edef0e463" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380672}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "76ab55a5-b7ae-513b-bef2-54d7a5d78561" :question/math-academy-id "q-122660" :question/problem "Determine the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4})\\,i+\\frac{xy}{x-1}\\,j\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4}),\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{xy}{x-1}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4=0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 4}.\n$$\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx-1=0\\,⟹\\,x=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $Q$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ne 1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 4,\\,x\\ne 1}\\,.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380685 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ac4dc2b-7925-5bb6-a8d7-26be528ca51e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/59/59ec29347dea9437c8217a208bde0a8b200e37fcbce9c223d0358011a0685297.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380687} {:answer/id #uuid "de160c2f-72c8-5594-b423-821d641cf29b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/27/27f151bdd3d619de0988b786e6b7517a7f7586844f9a7a35bf651063e7a7dfd1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380688} {:answer/id #uuid "fc0e97a0-e2b2-5364-a798-7ba629c78a40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fa/fadd7ae69d155ebf23586b88369da8f6624b1919942dad543917592f2d3c195c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380689} {:answer/id #uuid "7de70ea1-561a-5260-a36d-8f1436c265b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cb/cb10aa8f4d930ec7422da7dedfa0650244dceb01afe7cc849ed2bb16ffd3e44d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380690} {:answer/id #uuid "72aa0994-f928-5da5-8344-fae3471548d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a1/a1ee85bca08b21494e6f1f47de3abcbd71d2d01117795ef91d8ee3e038b36271.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de160c2f-72c8-5594-b423-821d641cf29b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/27/27f151bdd3d619de0988b786e6b7517a7f7586844f9a7a35bf651063e7a7dfd1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380688} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab0fa5e8-8931-50fe-a99e-b67ba6230eec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380686}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e932ad34-5aaf-594d-8943-da2547f2acb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-122677" :question/problem "Determine which of the following shaded regions depicts the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y-x}}\\,i+\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,j\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y-x}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\ny-x>0\\,⟹\\,y>x.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{1},$ shown below:\n\n![](images/c7/c78759bc8f9926159065129e00c1fc8b4341ab83ad40cc87e67a2e82b3e6b089.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive or equal to zero. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n2-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 2.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{2},$ which is a circle of radius $\\sqrt{2},$ as shown below:\n\n![](images/bd/bdf02ff330f256327d0c9f1147c388b25184610b36ea7ab77bb2c570ec3be19a.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by ${D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2},$ shown below.\n\n![](images/0d/0d3180a60e33c05e904c8fc00fdec4197510198fef3de3fa8c7b3549609205f6.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387222 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d59160e7-9169-5e83-a5c4-ad0518caff28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/21/2132ac0140fb0b96a876fd04a1069d6922662c72d4d8f4b8880eacae458f8181.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387224} {:answer/id #uuid "d5e66ef4-c389-503c-adac-bf6a7d3d4bc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fe/fefa684ba6cc6b2e8060c8b0860295177bade694786439317d6cee3d8cf51da8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387225} {:answer/id #uuid "1a97f507-5b03-5608-9bf1-7d0e5956f463" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/55/559ffda45a088810dcaa81628b8767bfedc64ca7f6b7ebd82aebdb2ed4a7ba00.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387226} {:answer/id #uuid "e78dff93-70e1-5457-996d-4e768112af8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/45/459b65c41ef9535d55703a64f3c9e4bfe9ffd9263a6a0f5dbf68405fb22c347d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387227} {:answer/id #uuid "0f013635-08f3-5ce8-b2d1-e6ffd2a329b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9a/9a20235e26590dbc6ddc5bdaaaa5dd484f5caf02e510bfabf2139ed7add5fa2d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387228}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a97f507-5b03-5608-9bf1-7d0e5956f463" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/55/559ffda45a088810dcaa81628b8767bfedc64ca7f6b7ebd82aebdb2ed4a7ba00.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387226} :answer-field/id #uuid "6702c2d4-7e00-59d1-8dcc-92a8c2e4e69d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387223}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "57d2166f-4843-548f-9e93-704a19813c47" :question/math-academy-id "q-122554" :question/problem "Determine which of the following shaded regions depicts the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{x-y}\\,j\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{x-y}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n3-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<3.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{1},$ which is the interior of a circle of radius $\\sqrt{3},$ as shown below:\n\n![](images/f9/f9c3a22a56839165bfd6c3b05f78ca25053553532063192836d0332d2b070436.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx-y=0\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{1},$ which is the entire plane ${R}^{2}$ with the line $y=x$ removed:\n\n![](images/a1/a1ef93cc90bdd59b922723c2c2aa2bd670b43e416923892c9db53b36ac6b7e57.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by ${D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2},$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f7/f70cab5ec58e63e1751faa65b575a20da748b3e8ee90cffac8f1f3b029bbbd79.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398235 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a308d91e-d998-5724-b79b-60e732336f24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x<{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398237} {:answer/id #uuid "c7ad4611-2a25-5515-9a96-aa614194aed2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398238} {:answer/id #uuid "185f7409-0599-54ba-a0cd-ab79287fc960" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>{y}^{2},\\,x\\ne 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398239} {:answer/id #uuid "c6f293d2-0d9e-5145-8206-ea5780ccb464" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398240} {:answer/id #uuid "689f2980-5408-56a2-a1df-2b0d8b222552" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>{y}^{2},\\,x>1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398241}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6f293d2-0d9e-5145-8206-ea5780ccb464" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398240} :answer-field/id #uuid "48ae7e1a-6a16-5754-8819-ca18faede750" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398236}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f9d1b0c7-3a1c-573d-9729-ab5857b0ca01" :question/math-academy-id "q-122663" :question/problem "Determine the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-{y}^{2})\\,i+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}){e}^{1/x}\\,j\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-{y}^{2}),\\,Q(x,y)=({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}){e}^{1/x}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the logarithm is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\nx-{y}^{2}>0\\,⟹\\,x>{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator of the exponent vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx=0\\,⟹\\,x=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>{y}^{2}}\\,.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain of a Vector Field"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172087 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171893 :question/id #uuid "61c3506a-5e4e-557f-bb8c-077170e93204" :question/math-academy-id "e-9468" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^3?$\n\n- $\\mathbf R(x,y,z) = 2\\,\\mathbf i - x\\,\\mathbf j + y\\,\\mathbf k$\n\n- $\\mathbf S(x,y) = 2x\\,\\mathbf i - y^3\\,\\mathbf j$\n\n- $\\mathbf T(x,y,z) = \\dfrac{x}{z}\\,\\mathbf i - \\dfrac{y}{z}\\,\\mathbf j + \\dfrac{z}{2}\\,\\mathbf k$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^3.$ A vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ is a function $\\mathbf F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $\\mathbf R(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z) \\in \\mathbb{R^3}$ to a vector in $\\mathbb{R^3}.$\n\n- The function $\\mathbf S(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns points in $\\mathbb R^2$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$) to vectors in $\\mathbb R^2$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$).\n\n- The function $\\mathbf T(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS = \\big\\{(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3\\:|\\: z \\neq 0\\big\\}\n$$\n\nto a vector in $\\mathbb R^3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb34fdb0-198e-507d-a944-9be782b4cfeb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172279 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d015c74-c5e0-5aef-8e09-1283f57ea5af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254199} {:answer/id #uuid "affd561e-af4d-58e2-bfbe-6320f0af0e42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255217} {:answer/id #uuid "cc771a9b-2a75-5e58-aac2-d970f8b52f78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255488} {:answer/id #uuid "d7127032-5df6-55a6-9301-b047e0321c13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255574} {:answer/id #uuid "d7d49e1c-5bde-5ec4-858a-e260cc5c430c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "affd561e-af4d-58e2-bfbe-6320f0af0e42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255217} :answer-field/id #uuid "5870a3e5-dc79-5eea-83fe-4894d64a3cdc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256091}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "060c20fc-1491-5624-9b50-89ab0dfe4d22" :question/math-academy-id "q-122467" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $G(t)={e}^{t}\\,i-t\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k$\n- $H(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j$\n- $K(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,i-y\\,j+z\\,k$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $G(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $H(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $K(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,x\\ne n\\pi ,\\,n\\in Z}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172285 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1acf9295-80b0-516b-96f6-ea57ff418e00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253899} {:answer/id #uuid "6341e0f1-f66f-533c-8750-dfabab18ba1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254561} {:answer/id #uuid "65f0a3f4-ea32-5d95-ad27-aa9702523a1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254583} {:answer/id #uuid "ad9f4d9c-d4cb-5c25-8f52-0736b7c72fd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255202} {:answer/id #uuid "cf1bc398-9956-525c-883f-70b34e987e00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6341e0f1-f66f-533c-8750-dfabab18ba1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254561} :answer-field/id #uuid "4eda6f1e-9be0-5856-a2d2-8abe38bb2111" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256076}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4052d0d0-adca-5eb3-9bef-49295b1e5651" :question/math-academy-id "q-122697" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $U(x,y,z)=⟨x+2,\\sqrt{x-y},\\operatorname{ln}⁡z⟩$\n- $V(x,y)=⟨x{e}^{y},1-y⟩$\n- $W(t)=⟨{t}^{2},2t,2-3t⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $U(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,x\\ge y,\\,z>0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $V(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $W(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256694 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a0e2775-dae5-5363-a311-a301ca37db9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253890} {:answer/id #uuid "530f8e39-cb60-5d6f-9021-1268081d55d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254396} {:answer/id #uuid "54c2ae82-1e33-50b6-9b45-93678c89f1fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254409} {:answer/id #uuid "63c6f01c-573e-5a49-ba25-157919c201cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254565} {:answer/id #uuid "9062563e-0f92-548e-b519-03e516bdf7b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54c2ae82-1e33-50b6-9b45-93678c89f1fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254409} :answer-field/id #uuid "28fa9082-3c30-531c-bc27-0dc4d10a64e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255992}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "72c8972f-c700-5c53-9029-c1e143b47247" :question/math-academy-id "q-122685" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $T(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j+{z}^{2}\\,k$\n- $U(t)=2t\\,i-{t}^{2}\\,j-\\sqrt{t}\\,k$\n- $V(x,y,z)={e}^{-2z}\\sqrt{x+y}$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $T(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}$ to a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $U(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $V(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{3}$ to functions in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256695 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39b861f2-69ec-5c65-9508-f7294f45044a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254175} {:answer/id #uuid "72c370f1-172c-5c9f-95ad-985b95828365" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254687} {:answer/id #uuid "770cf01b-45a0-56c4-9961-b8699f72d11f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254724} {:answer/id #uuid "a69d8038-8063-5f8c-8998-13ee7e4f1213" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255138} {:answer/id #uuid "f9a7d368-1751-561c-a4a6-3b4918ec23a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255857}] :