swer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253886} {:answer/id #uuid "4282ef58-c196-533e-a6c5-65a78e4167e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254237} {:answer/id #uuid "72371778-9fa6-55d9-a8c5-e39c0c1c76f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254682} {:answer/id #uuid "81960d80-10de-550c-8912-2cf1586a9e49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254816} {:answer/id #uuid "eed3a33f-1dad-5c80-bfdb-c8efe6f43c69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eed3a33f-1dad-5c80-bfdb-c8efe6f43c69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255760} :answer-field/id #uuid "74382c67-5fd5-5692-9423-b4141bf3106f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256135}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "659f4527-cc88-5c6b-8485-f595eee1689f" :question/math-academy-id "q-122694" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $N(x,y)=⟨x{e}^{y},\\sqrt{x+y}⟩$\n- $P(x,y,z)=⟨{e}^{x-2y},\\sqrt{3-x},3{z}^{3}⟩$\n- $Q(x,y,z)=⟨-1,2,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x-y+z)⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $N(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $P(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,3-x\\ge 0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $Q(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,2x-y+z>0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256699 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32dc221c-9d3c-5ba5-b8f8-5f66cc98c204" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254109} {:answer/id #uuid "83efb2f1-1659-564d-b42b-e819c7758c66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254843} {:answer/id #uuid "9493d975-f804-58ac-949a-37a70ebd19d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254975} {:answer/id #uuid "9e63abe1-bc16-5e58-8947-238f06b6a38a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255064} {:answer/id #uuid "db43c005-6e48-5fe7-a0a7-8355e9b574ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255615}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83efb2f1-1659-564d-b42b-e819c7758c66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254843} :answer-field/id #uuid "61039999-72de-5aa0-b04f-7ff4d69d025f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256102}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "89ed8a40-4bb8-5a40-9a2c-1bf03637a8bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-122696" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $C(x,y,z)=\\frac{z}{x+y}$\n- $D(x,y)=⟨{y}^{3}-3,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y),7{x}^{3}⟩$\n- $E(x,y,z)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+z),\\operatorname{tan}⁡z,\\frac{1}{x-y}⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $C(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{3}$ to functions in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $D(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $E(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,x\\ne y,\\,z\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\\,n\\in Z}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380692 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e1241cb-e0b2-5b46-8f65-a3c4c3647e6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380694} {:answer/id #uuid "81271eb6-6f13-53c5-8aa9-8681bb11bee7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380695} {:answer/id #uuid "cd3f72cb-d68f-5920-a038-03163c1fef91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380696} {:answer/id #uuid "2e773405-32c0-5805-81d2-1ac74b422fb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380697} {:answer/id #uuid "2ce4f059-c075-5696-ac0c-73923ae49957" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ce4f059-c075-5696-ac0c-73923ae49957" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380698} :answer-field/id #uuid "26e476e2-9bb5-503b-95c4-0d2fc7849f6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380693}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b17979a9-57b7-5c2d-8065-c277267e307e" :question/math-academy-id "q-122686" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $P(x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,i+(x+y)^{2}\\,j$\n- $Q(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i-\\frac{x}{y}\\,j+\\frac{1}{z+1}\\,k$\n- $R(t)=\\frac{1}{t+1}\\,i-(3t-1)\\,j+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,k$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $Q(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,z\\ne -1,\\,y\\ne 0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $R(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380699 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7ef600e-3fa7-5dd9-a9fc-2b417fe03e74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380701} {:answer/id #uuid "5e6ec70d-19a0-5b3e-bfe1-e05b2b422ef3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380702} {:answer/id #uuid "75de271f-3b44-5352-b40a-86d6328346d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380703} {:answer/id #uuid "49cd7923-2c07-57ef-bd86-d4b6461a7cf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380704} {:answer/id #uuid "f5e3f847-6c34-58ac-ab31-4ffb21d98290" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380705}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e6ec70d-19a0-5b3e-bfe1-e05b2b422ef3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380702} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fa44fa7-67a0-5e32-9d16-0c65707b3d44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380700}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "438dc864-2346-5ec7-8b61-791bfb4d3c82" :question/math-academy-id "q-122695" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $M(x,y,z)=⟨z-{x}^{2},3x+{z}^{2},\\frac{1}{x+y}⟩$\n- $N(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+1,1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x⟩$\n- $P(t)=⟨{t}^{3},\\sqrt{1-t}\\,⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $M(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,x+y\\ne 0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $N(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $P(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields on Three-Dimensions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172088 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171894 :question/id #uuid "b226c391-9538-59ff-85ff-dde4d73f209b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9473" :question/problem "![](images/de/de305db47bcfae58b75797e88ae4a7c7825174bd58d77991ba8dfb4c0b8a13cb.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?\n\n- $\\mathbf  F(x,y,z) = \\mathbf j + \\mathbf k$\n\n- $\\mathbf  G(x,y,z) = \\mathbf k$\n\n- $\\mathbf  H(x,y,z) = z\\,\\mathbf k$\n\n- $\\mathbf  K(x,y,z) = -\\mathbf k$" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $z$-axis. So, the $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\mathbf j + \\mathbf k\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $z$-direction, so the $\\mathbf k$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf G(x,y,z) = \\mathbf k\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $\\mathbf i$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf H(x,y,z) = z\\,\\mathbf k\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf K(x,y,z) = -\\mathbf k.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "888aa30d-7179-507c-a30c-38209587333b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172282 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0af393b5-0b0a-51ee-963e-9232b624b8d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "G(x,y,z)=-i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253755} {:answer/id #uuid "4ebe45a6-6f14-5783-a2c5-0fff74a623ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K(x,y,z)={e}^{x}\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254352} {:answer/id #uuid "5a235ec0-b137-57da-bcd7-fa7e7f95a670" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H(x,y,z)=i+j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254460} {:answer/id #uuid "6c27c496-3691-52cb-89b6-a510f5ec6427" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(x,y,z)=i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254631} {:answer/id #uuid "99ad8cef-0db5-5109-bbb4-eb38f07c8dc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c27c496-3691-52cb-89b6-a510f5ec6427" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(x,y,z)=i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254631} :answer-field/id #uuid "1226930a-6b24-57fb-a5e7-1829039d9728" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255946}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "505eb075-afb5-5225-b846-447773bcc89d" :question/math-academy-id "q-122559" :question/problem "![](images/cf/cfc15b6202167211b7d6c0e8f6635dd9e571810d5296a209a4c317350604c4a4.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $x$-axis. So, the $j$ and $k$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nH(x,y,z)=i+j+k,\\,\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *positive* $x$-direction, so the $i$ component of our vector field is positive. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=-i\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $i$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y,z)={e}^{x}\\,i\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $F(x,y,z)=i.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172284 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48f0b5a2-3be5-594f-a754-99c98d96988e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254295} {:answer/id #uuid "c4981513-4d32-54f0-884a-7cfa95b9c2fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H(x,y,z)=-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255427} {:answer/id #uuid "d6dc3e8c-9e7f-50a4-b937-a99d9f06d7ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "G(x,y,z)=i-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255572} {:answer/id #uuid "da52d501-6db0-512c-804d-9bbd6ce2ad8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255607} {:answer/id #uuid "fbbc53f3-143a-5ed2-b73c-2af527f6a4cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K(x,y,z)=2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255881}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbbc53f3-143a-5ed2-b73c-2af527f6a4cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K(x,y,z)=2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255881} :answer-field/id #uuid "a548685d-413d-5f58-8841-0b64dd56f823" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256224}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0777fbfe-65b4-5afe-92ce-361fbbf5b37f" :question/math-academy-id "q-122664" :question/problem "![](images/7a/7a0a919af689e9c332ba6ceb3d606208dab9eabadef9b4871ac8e164843ebe88.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $z$-axis. So, the $i$ and $j$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=i-k\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *positive* $z$-direction, so the $k$ component of our vector field is positive. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nH(x,y,z)=-k\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $k$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,k\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $K(x,y,z)=2\\,k.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256686 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f6c3873-4d1d-574a-a9c2-8b7237391726" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(x,y,z)=i+x\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253944} {:answer/id #uuid "77a79fac-2c83-5559-8e88-df7821819c38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254728} {:answer/id #uuid "7cc3b387-dc15-5110-8f89-a3ced8863a54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "G(x,y,z)=-i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254776} {:answer/id #uuid "a7b92edb-bdc2-5822-8401-8722052a64cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K(x,y,z)=2\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255150} {:answer/id #uuid "efac23a6-983f-5fcf-b670-3454bacfa6f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H(x,y,z)=x\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7b92edb-bdc2-5822-8401-8722052a64cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K(x,y,z)=2\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255150} :answer-field/id #uuid "41312e99-823c-57ca-bbea-af3327d42ce6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256044}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b83bfdca-4e4b-517b-aad8-aed17555d2d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-122651" :question/problem "![](images/7c/7cb4dd61f9d42a48c022a183e7f4310a8a4ed7ae881edd136881ef6863282e2c.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $x$-axis. So, the $j$ and $k$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=i+x\\,j\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *positive* $x$-direction, so the $i$ component of our vector field is positive. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=-i\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $i$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nH(x,y,z)=x\\,i\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $K(x,y,z)=2\\,i.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256689 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "246c2163-a431-53df-9c32-91cc7baa04f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K(x,y,z)=2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253998} {:answer/id #uuid "664169a1-99e9-5129-a9ac-60cb4c0afd03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H(x,y,z)=x\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254588} {:answer/id #uuid "66f7f9c9-6005-52af-8503-4204ea104780" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "G(x,y,z)=4\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254599} {:answer/id #uuid "ce110d77-de29-50d0-b4a3-b3cd16e56e9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255501} {:answer/id #uuid "ce8e27bb-e7f4-5967-96b3-21b1a4efd7bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(x,y,z)=-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255506}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce8e27bb-e7f4-5967-96b3-21b1a4efd7bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(x,y,z)=-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255506} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf119d84-49c7-595f-9a0e-210df016ca2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256261}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "db25e593-34c2-554c-af14-c5a728ea4219" :question/math-academy-id "q-122668" :question/problem "![](images/f8/f89653698d9c2bad0c2e6a77d4dad1953a9fd72f89cbfc08fd68aac802acd284.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $y$-axis. So, the $i$ and $k$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=4\\,k\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $y$-direction, so the $j$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y,z)=2\\,j\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $j$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nH(x,y,z)=x\\,j\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $F(x,y,z)=-j.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256690 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52540afe-6bae-5db6-95a2-a9867a8f858b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417332} {:answer/id #uuid "52693f37-1a7d-5506-8ee4-6500aa2d7349" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(x,y,z)=i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417333} {:answer/id #uuid "c2ec2c00-c765-5813-aaa4-789423b68551" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H(x,y,z)=k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417336} {:answer/id #uuid "f6c9d1db-461c-5c21-9d19-a6e9f1533a90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "G(x,y,z)=-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417339} {:answer/id #uuid "ff5ef832-2edf-5758-ba7c-c1096ad3a8c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K(x,y,z)=-{x}^{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417340}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52540afe-6bae-5db6-95a2-a9867a8f858b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417332} :answer-field/id #uuid "f30ffaea-faee-50f8-bb72-c0bdccf87c9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417342}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "548bbae5-603d-5bc6-9f7d-4ca6dbc971eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-122670" :question/problem "![](images/64/64f02fa7dcd23b20e61801c0453d8fe7ed013936572e1ee63f748361edd4dec5.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $z$-axis. So, the $i$ and $j$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=i,\\,G(x,y,z)=-j\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $z$-direction, so the $k$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nH(x,y,z)=k\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $k$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y,z)=-{x}^{2}\\,k\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram doesn't correspond to any of the listed vector fields."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259551 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44832c8e-8988-5ee0-ac8b-67ae439d923f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257381} {:answer/id #uuid "70a0b147-68a3-5ec0-ae90-b9c82057aad3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H(x,y,z)=-2\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257773} {:answer/id #uuid "96d0c94c-faa8-5d39-b3c3-d13382b0eced" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(x,y,z)=i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258114} {:answer/id #uuid "acdf99e3-cf16-5362-88ce-860873156446" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K(x,y,z)=-i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258289} {:answer/id #uuid "f8d361e0-0169-5758-b933-86a6